WEBVTT

00:00.100 --> 00:04.004
ORCAs generates robot demonstrations from reusable programs.

00:04.300 --> 00:12.612
Robot learning needs diverse demonstrations. But collecting them in the
physical world requires repeated human effort and scene resets.

00:12.900 --> 00:21.635
Simulation can reduce that burden. Prior work such as MimicGen expands seed
demonstrations, yet new tasks still need reliable motions.

00:21.900 --> 00:30.494
To create those motions, agentic frameworks offer a starting point: write
code, test execution, and improve robot behavior through feedback.

00:30.800 --> 00:34.765
ORCAs applies this idea, starting with a task and prepared simulator.

00:35.500 --> 00:40.733
Coding agents then develop a planning program, testing and repairing it in
simulation.

00:41.300 --> 00:46.573
Once validated, the frozen program generates demonstrations across new initial
states.

00:47.200 --> 00:50.481
Those demonstrations train a policy for the real robot.

00:51.100 --> 00:54.990
To build that program, the orchestrator first reads the task and tools.

00:55.500 --> 00:58.652
It assigns specialists to develop or repair the components.

00:59.700 --> 01:02.612
Specialists generate the required planning components.

01:03.100 --> 01:06.785
These include PDDL models and Python motion samplers.

01:07.200 --> 01:10.920
Simulation tests validate results; failures trigger repairs.

01:11.300 --> 01:14.286
Validated artifacts enter the archive for reuse.

01:14.700 --> 01:22.086
We first test generalization on LIBERO-PRO: thirty tasks, two perturbation
types, and three thousand held-out evaluations.

01:22.400 --> 01:27.854
The tests also expose missed placements, control timeouts, and tasks with no
valid program.

01:28.200 --> 01:37.337
Even with these failures, mean success reaches ninety-two point seven percent.
Removing adaptive orchestration lowers it to eighty-two point three percent.

01:37.600 --> 01:45.348
We then move beyond rigid-object tasks, testing deformable manipulation and
contact-rich assembly under a shared token budget.

01:45.600 --> 01:52.322
Starting with deformable objects, rope manipulation succeeds in twenty-six of
thirty held-out evaluation states.

01:52.600 --> 01:58.094
The teddy task is harder: lifting and placing the bear succeeds in twelve of
thirty held-out states.

01:59.600 --> 02:06.262
For precision assembly, peg insertion performs strongly, succeeding in twenty-
nine out of thirty evaluation trials.

02:06.600 --> 02:13.121
Gear meshing is similarly reliable, with twenty-eight successes out of thirty
trials across varied initial states.

02:13.600 --> 02:21.208
The archive also helps with new tasks, expanding coverage from five to six
position tasks and seven to nine task variants.

02:21.500 --> 02:25.425
Finally, we test the full pipeline in a reconstructed real workspace.

02:25.700 --> 02:28.377
Here, the tasks are cube stacking and bowl stacking.

02:28.700 --> 02:35.040
Frozen programs then collect successful demonstrations in parallel, without
further calls to the coding models.

02:35.300 --> 02:42.666
One thousand demonstrations take ninety-six minutes for cubes and one hundred
twenty-four for bowls, including program development.

02:43.000 --> 02:50.708
Trained on this data, the policy achieves seventy-five percent success in cube
stacking. The footage is a teleoperation reference.

02:51.000 --> 02:59.775
Bowl stacking reaches ninety-five percent. Together, these results show how
reusable programs generate training data for real robot skills.
